 ## check.sh
 
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files still
+  only warn; the final `exit 0` is reached only when every step succeeded.
+- Forbidden: removing `-e`, or swallowing an internal error so that the script still ends in `exit 0`.
+- Reason: maintainer decision. Failing open hid breakage inside the check; nobody noticed when it stopped working.
+- Note: under `-e`, write conditionals as `if …; then …; fi`, not `test && action`. A failed `test` as the last command
+  in the loop body becomes the loop's status, and `pipefail` turns that into an abort with no output.
+
 ### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
 - Since: 2026-09-11
+- Superseded by §check-fails-closed on 2026-10-22.
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
 - Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
